de faster per mile than a rolling tire. **4. Thickness matters** Tire tread depth starts around 8–11 mm (often more for all-terrain). A shoe sole’s rubber portion might be 2–4 mm. So the tire simply has way more material to sacrifice before it’s “bald.” **Analogy: aluminum foil vs. a steel pan** Both are metal, but you wouldn’t expect foil to survive years of scraping and flipping burgers because it’s engineered for a different job. Same with rubber — it’s a whole category, not one substance. So your dad’s hunch isn’t crazy; it’s just that everyday language lumps together a huge variety of “rubber” products. Once you account for composition, construction, and the different ways we abuse shoes vs. tires, it makes sense they age on totally different clocks.
