22:04:35 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I hear you—that’s a totally reasonable suspicion. When an explanation gets complicated, it can start to sound like an excuse for “they just don’t make ’em like they used to.” But this isn’t a case of shoe companies maliciously cutting corners. It’s a genuine, unavoidable trade-off that you can test yourself. Let me break it down without the jargon. ### 1. Shoe companies *do* make “tire-tough” soles when they need to Work boots, hiking boots, and some military boots often have thick, hard rubber outsoles—much closer to tire compounds. They can last years of brutal use. So the capability is there. The question is: why don’t all shoes get that? ### 2. The real reason: comfort and function, not cost-cutting A shoe’s job isn’t just “don’t wear out.” It’s to support, cushion, and flex with your foot. If you put a thick, stiff, tire-like slab of rubber under your foot: - **It’s heavy as hell.** Walking in heavy soles is like dragging a mini tire on each foot—your legs fatigue faster. - **It’s miserably inflexible.** Your foot wants to bend at the ball, but a thick, dense rubber slab resists. You end up clomping along, and the shoe fights your natural stride. - **It loses cushioning.** The squishy, energy-returning foam that protects your joints can’t be replaced by hard rubber. You’d feel every impact. So a “tire-tough” street shoe would be uncomfortable, clunky, and exhausting to walk in for daily use. When companies do make them (some work boots), they’re explicitly targeting durability over all-day walking comfort. ### 3. The physics isn’t an excuse; it’s the mechanism Even if you made a sneaker sole from exactly the same rubber as a high-end tire, **walking would still wipe it out faster per mile than rolling** because: - **Walking involves sliding and twisting shear forces** that literally grind the rubber away. Rolling tires experience almost none of that. - **Pressure**: Your body weight concentrated on a small heel strike exerts far more pounds per square inch of abrasion than a car’s weight spread over a large contact patch. - **Cycles**: Each step is a whole new flex, squash, and scrub. That mechanical fatigue adds up. Imagine taking a car tire off the rim, grabbing it like a huge sandal, and dragging it across the pavement with your body weight—it would shred just as fast as a shoe sole, probably faster because you’re gouging it. Rolling protects it. ### 4. The “planned obsolescence” suspicion It’s easy to think companies love it when you buy shoes every 6–12 months. And they’re not exactly sad about it. But if it were purely greed, some startup would have swooped in with “indestructible tires for your feet” and captured the market. Every few years someone tries—and the shoes are always panned as uncomfortable bricks that nobody wants to wear. The market has spoken: people prioritize lightness, style, and cushioning over extreme longevity. ### Putting it together So you’re not wrong to feel like shoes *could* last longer. They can—look at your hiking boots. But they’d be terrible at everything a daily shoe is supposed to do. The physics of walking is harsh, and shoe companies aren’t cutting corners so much as navigating a genuine materials-science dilemma: **light, cushioned, flexible** vs. **indestructible**. You can only pick two.
